@*
    One row in a message list, in the template's own mail idiom.

    The class names here are not decoration — smartapp.min.css already styles them:

        .unread .mail-sender, .unread .mail-subject   { font-weight: 600 }
        .unread .mail-body                            { color: var(--bs-body-color) !important }

    So unread state is expressed by putting `unread` on the row and letting the stylesheet do the
    rest, rather than by conditionally applying our own weights. That is the whole reason to adopt
    the template's markup rather than approximate its appearance: the styling already exists, and
    matching it means our messages look like the rest of the product instead of nearly like it.

    The responsive shape is the template's too — sender, subject and snippet sit on one line on a
    wide screen and stack on a narrow one, with `order-*` moving the timestamp out of the way. That
    behaviour comes free with the classes; reproducing it by hand is how a list ends up unreadable
    on a phone.

    This must be placed inside a `<ul class="notification">`, which is where the rest of the row's
    appearance lives:

        .notification li          { background: var(--bs-body-bg) }
        .notification li.unread   { background: rgba(var(--bs-primary-rgb), .05) }
        .notification li > :first-child { padding: .75rem 1.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid … }

    Note that last selector: the separator and padding are applied to the row's *first child*, so
    the outer div below has to stay the first thing in the `<li>`. Adding a sibling ahead of it
    (a checkbox column, say) moves the border onto that sibling instead.
*@

<li class="@(Unread ? "unread" : null)">
    <div class="d-flex align-items-center px-3 px-sm-4 py-2 py-lg-0 height-4 height-mobile-auto gap-2
                mail-row @(Selected ? "mail-row--selected" : null)"
         role="button" tabindex="0"
         @onclick="OnOpen"
         @onkeydown="HandleKeyAsync">

        @* The badge column: a channel or audience pill, so a row says who it went to. The template
           has a star here; ours has the thing a reader actually needs to know about a message. *@
        <div class="d-flex align-self-center order-3 order-lg-2 me-lg-3 me-0 flex-shrink-0">
            @Badge
        </div>

        <div class="d-flex flex-column flex-lg-row flex-grow-1 align-items-stretch order-2 order-lg-3"
             style="min-width: 0;">

            <div class="mail-sender flex-shrink-0 align-self-start align-self-lg-center width-sm width-max-sm text-truncate">
                @Sender
            </div>

            <div class="d-flex flex-column flex-lg-row flex-grow-1 w-100 overflow-hidden">
                <div class="mail-subject flex-shrink-0 align-self-start align-self-lg-center me-2 text-truncate width-max-100">
                    @(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Subject) ? "(no subject)" : Subject)
                </div>

                <div class="d-flex align-items-center flex-grow-1 w-100 overflow-hidden">
                    <div class="mail-body d-block text-truncate w-100 pe-lg-6 text-muted">
                        <span class="hidden-sm">-</span>
                        <MessageBody Body="@Body" Html="@BodyIsHtml" Snippet="true" />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="fs-sm text-muted ms-auto order-4 position-on-mobile-absolute pos-top pos-right
                    mt-2 me-3 me-sm-4 mt-lg-0 me-lg-0 flex-shrink-0">
            @When
        </div>
    </div>
</li>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Sender { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public string? Subject { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string Body { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    [Parameter] public bool BodyIsHtml { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Already formatted — the row does not decide how a date reads.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? When { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Drives the template's own bolding. See the note above.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Unread { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Highlights the row whose message is open beside it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Selected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The pill shown ahead of the sender — channel, audience, whatever the list means.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? Badge { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnOpen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Enter and Space open the row.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The row is a div carrying role="button", which gives it the right name and role for a
    /// screen reader but none of a real button's behaviour — a keyboard user would be able to
    /// focus it and unable to open it. A button element cannot be used instead: this markup is the
    /// template's, and it nests block-level flex containers a button may not contain.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task HandleKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key is "Enter" or " ") await OnOpen.InvokeAsync();
    }
}
